  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1212 AM CDT Tue Jun 16 2026\n\nThe hottest days of the summer so far are coming up Wednesday and \nThursday. With lee surface trough development to our west and a \ntightening pressure gradient, breezy south winds are expected on \nWednesday. Forecast highs Wednesday are in the mid to upper 90s in \nmuch of the southeastern half of our area, and 100-105 in much of \nthe Big Country and Concho Valley. Highs at a couple of locations in \nthe western Big Country may exceed 105. Heat indices will climb to \n103-106 in a good portion of the Big Country/Concho Valley. Heat \nAdvisory looks a little more marginal on Wednesday, but may be \nneeded for portions of the Big Country and Concho Valley. \n\nThursday looks to be the hottest day, when a mid/upper level \nridge shifts east into Texas from the southwestern states. A \nsurface low is forecast to be near Lubbock on Thursday morning, \nand then move southeast toward our northwestern counties in the \nafternoon. While there is some uncertainty on the specifics of the\nsetup (and just how hot temperatures will get), current\nindications are for a dryline (extending south from this low) to \nmix east to our far western counties by mid-afternoon. Temperatures\nbehind the dryline will soar to 105-109 degrees. Ahead of the \ndryline, highs are expected to reach 100-104 in the southeastern \nhalf of our area, and 105-108 along and west of a line from Anson \nto San Angelo to Ozona. A cold front should move south into the \nnorthwestern Big Country by late afternoon/early evening. Given \nthe forecast highs and with heat indices expected to reach 105- \n110 in most of our area, a Heat Advisory will likely be needed. \nWill have to assess further (confidence is lower) if an Excessive \nHeat Warning may be needed for any part of our area. \n\nOur area has low to medium chances (mostly 25-50 percent) of showers \nand thunderstorms Thursday night and Friday. The cold front is \nforecast to move south across our area Thursday night/early Friday.\nMay also have a weak upper level disturbance move southeast across \nthe area. A moist airmass is progged once again with precipitable \nwater values increasing to 1.5 to 2 inches. This supports a \npotential for locally heavy rain. With increased cloud cover, \ntemperatures are expected to be cooler on Friday with highs limited \nto the upper 80s to lower 90s. A possibility of isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms lingers into Saturday. Hotter \ntemperatures to return for Sunday into the first part of next week, \nwhen upper level ridging is progged to expand over the Gulf Coast \nand west across Texas.",
